  "live_records": {
    "q-36825": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36825",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $Q(1,1)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $Q$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =\\frac{2(1)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the $1$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2y & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $Q(1,1)$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
