    "q-71412": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71412",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14264187/assets/a3e1daa4db558edc65d65f6bfe25d848de6499fa47695011fa3fb7afda326c2e.png)\n\nWhich of the following could represent the level curves of the surface $z=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$ shown above?",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the range of $f(x,y)$ is $[0,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider only $c$-level curves for $c\\ge 0.$\n\nThe level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equations \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =c \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the equations $y=\\pm \\sqrt{2c}$ do not depend on $x.$ So, for a given $c\\ge 0$, the right-hand side of the equation is constant. As a result, we obtain a family of horizontal lines, as shown below.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14264187/assets/9f243d14e996f643e746a2c20c4e606df94420355d7da6211770265af2883619.png)",
