    "q-128451": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128451",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Determine the equation of the level curve that passes through the point $P(2,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,0) & =\\frac{1}{2-{0}^{2}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2-0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the $\\frac{1}{5}$-level curve.\n\nRecall that the level curves for the function $f(x,y)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{1}{5}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{x-{y}^{2}+3} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5 & =x-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ x & ={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level curve that passes through $P(2,0)$ is the parabola $x={y}^{2}+2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
